{"questions":[{"question_id":"nrbseZ6OuEZem1EX","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2004","year":2004,"examDate":2004,"subject":"physics","chapter":"dual-nature-of-radiation","chapterGroup":"modern-physics","topic":"photoelectric-effect","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"according-to-einsteins-photoelectric-equation-the-plot-of-2004-marks-4-nrbsez6ouezem1ex.htm","content":"According to Einstein's photoelectric equation, the plot of the kinetic energy of the emitted photo electrons from a metal <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.801ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 1238 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D449\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D449\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(769,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D460\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D460\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> the frequency, of the incident radiation gives as straight the whose slope ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"depends both on the intensity of the radiation and the metal used"},{"identifier":"B","content":"depends on the intensity of the radiation "},{"identifier":"C","content":"depends on the nature of the metal used "},{"identifier":"D","content":"is the same for the all metals and independent of the intensity of the radiation "}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"<p>Einstein's photoelectric equation is given by</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"15.348ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 6783.9 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-6D\"></use><use data-c=\"61\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-61\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"78\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-78\" transform=\"translate(1333,0)\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2525.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(3581.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(4157.5,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4909.7,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(5909.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6505.9,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>where <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.086ex\" height=\"1.902ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2247.9 840.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-6D\"></use><use data-c=\"61\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-61\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"78\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-78\" transform=\"translate(1333,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.303ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 576 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is Planck's constant, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.005ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.199ex\" height=\"1.005ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 530 444\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is the frequency of the incident radiation, and <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.348ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 596 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> is the work function of the metal (the minimum energy needed to eject an electron).</p>\n<p>If you plot <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.357ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.086ex\" height=\"1.902ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2247.9 840.8\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"6D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-6D\"></use><use data-c=\"61\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-61\" transform=\"translate(833,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"78\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-78\" transform=\"translate(1333,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> against <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.005ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.199ex\" height=\"1.005ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 530 444\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D708\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D708\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>, you will get a straight line with slope <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.303ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 576 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (Planck's constant) and y-intercept <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.109ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 1374 899\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (the negative of the work function). The slope of the line (which is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.303ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -694 576 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) does not depend on the intensity of the radiation or the type of metal used. Instead, it is a universal constant. </p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is:</p>\n<p>Option D: The slope is the same for all metals and independent of the intensity of the radiation.</p>\n"}]}